{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SYopCy3o7bvezKeawmo24q","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Yamale schema for the scenario registry (path from\n2\t# `documents.scenarios_yaml` in true-bdd.yaml, conventionally\n3\t# docs/scenarios.yaml).\n4\t#\n5\t# The filename is not free: the schema gate pairs\n6\t# `true-bdd/<key>-schema.yaml` with `documents.<key>`, and the key is\n7\t# `scenarios_yaml` — the `_yaml` suffix included.\n8\t#\n9\t#   yamale -s true-bdd/scenarios_yaml-schema.yaml docs/scenarios.yaml\n10\t#\n11\t# Unlike the other two documents, this one IS parsed by the engine\n12\t# (`build tests` walks it) and by the test harness\n13\t# (pkg/testkit/bddgo runs it). So the schema's job is narrower than\n14\t# usual: it pins the shape those two readers already require, and its\n15\t# value is catching a hand-edit before a walk does.\n16\t#\n17\t# Three load-time rules the engine enforces that a schema cannot, worth\n18\t# knowing while editing this file:\n19\t#   - `scenarios:` must be a MAP keyed by id, never a list. A list is\n20\t#     well-formed YAML and fails only at decode.\n21\t#   - An empty map is a refusal, not a pass.\n22\t#   - A step is either a plain string or a one-key `{and: …}` / `{but: …}`\n23\t#     mapping; the modifier form is flattened to \"and <text>\".\n24\t#   - A step whose text opens with `llm:` or `judge:` is run by a model\n25\t#     rather than by a step definition, and each is legal in exactly one\n26\t#     place: `llm:` ACTS, so it belongs in given:/when:; `judge:` RULES\n27\t#     on what already happened, so it belongs in then: and nowhere else.\n28\t#     The prefix in the wrong block is a refusal, not a reinterpretation.\n29\t#     A `judge:` clause is collected as the scenario runs and ruled on\n30\t#     once at the end, so use it only for what no comparison can settle —\n31\t#     that two wordings mean the same thing, that a rewrite kept its\n32\t#     substance. Anything a step definition could assert belongs in one.\n33\t\n34\tmetadata: include('metadata')\n35\t# A key is its lineage id `<story-id>-<NNN>-<slug>` (ADR 0014), e.g.\n36\t# `scen-check-001-unknown-id-refused`; the retired `E2E-NNN` stays valid\n37\t# until each Story renames its Scenarios.\n38\tscenarios: map(include('scenario'), key=regex('^(E2E-[0-9]{3}|[a-z0-9][a-z0-9.-]*-[0-9]{3}-[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*)$'), min=1)\n39\t\n40\t---\n41\tmetadata:\n42\t  title: str()\n43\t  version: str()\n44\t  description: str()\n45\t  last_updated: str(required=False)\n46\t\n47\t---\n48\tscenario:\n49\t  # One sentence naming the behaviour, free of the words the product\n50\t  # document's `vocabulary:` forbids. The generated test carries it as the\n51\t  # comment above its function.\n52\t  description: str()\n53\t  # Must name a service declared in architecture.yaml. It is also the\n54\t  # join that decides which tests own the scenario: they live in\n55\t  # `tests/<service>/`, mirroring `services/<service>/`.\n56\t  service: str()\n57\t  # Generated test file this scenario is written into, repo-relative.\n58\t  # Several scenarios may share one file (emitted in id order). Required,\n59\t  # never defaulted — grouping is editorial judgement no generator should guess.\n60\t  path: str()\n61\t  # Free-form note on where the behaviour comes from. Optional.\n62\t  requirement: str(required=False)\n63\t  # Optional grouping label for a human reader; the engine never reads it.\n64\t  feature: str(required=False)\n65\t  last_updated: str(required=False)\n66\t  # Lineage back to the stories this scenario was merged from, written\n67\t  # by `us apply`. An empty list is valid and says so on the record: the\n68\t  # scenario exists and no story claims it yet.\n69\t  user_stories: list(include('user_story'))\n70\t  # How long the scenario's run may take, as a Go duration (\"10m\"); absent\n71\t  # means the suite's default. Not a step (a machine's speed isn't\n72\t  # behaviour) — an invalid duration